  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1255 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nSunday night through Friday:\n\nSynopsis: Settling in behind the Sunday front, the next area of high \npressure will move across the western Great Lakes during the Sunday \nnight to Tuesday morning time frame. Presence of the high will \npromote light surface winds and mostly clear skies for the beginning \nof the work week. The combination of sky and wind conditions will be \nfavorable for efficient radiational cooling both Sunday night and \nMonday night, with areas of frost possible. High pressure will \nvacate to the east of the area on Tuesday afternoon, with the next \nupper disturbance moving into the western Great Lakes Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday. Said disturbance will pull a cold front across \nsouthern Wisconsin either late Tuesday night or early Wednesday \nmorning. Precip appears unlikely along the front at this time. Mild, \nFall-like weather will prevail through the second half of the work \nweek behind the departing cold front.\n\nSunday night & Monday night: A newly-arrived Canadian air mass & \nsettling high pressure will create favorable conditions for \nefficient radiational cooling and chilly overnight lows. Current \nforecasts from the NBM point toward low temps in the upper 30s to \nnear 40 away from Lake Michigan, which appear reasonable for the \ntime-being given the set up. Will be monitoring for slight downward \nadjustments as this portion of the period draws closer, particularly \nin the traditionally cooler spots toward central Wisconsin, where \nprobabilistic guidance is indicating ~20% chances of overnight lows \nat or below 36. Given expected conditions, will need to watch for \nareas of frost during each overnight period. Whether or not any \nfrost will be widespread enough for headlines remains uncertain at \nthis time, with trends being monitored into early next week. Be sure \nto move any cold-sensitive plants indoors during each overnight \nperiod.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday morning: Will be monitoring for \npossible light rain chances along a passing cold front. Realization \nof precip will be fighting a number of limiting factors, including \nvery dry pre-frontal air & marginal frontal convergence/lift. Have \nthus continued a dry forecast in the mid-day update, which aligns \nwell with NBM precip probabilities near 5%. Will continue to \nevaluate for possible changes as this part of the period pulls \ncloser. \n\nQuigley",
